# Agda → Lean cross-prover return, and its verification status

## What was returned into the Lean package

The architectural pattern taken from the Agda side is:

> the residual does not need a favourable sign; the stable interface is
> `residual ≤ budget < strictSignalMargin`, together with its split form
> `structuredResidual + farResidual ≤ structuredBudget + farBudget < margin`.

Implemented, and **kernel-checked in this Lean project**:

- `ResidualBudgetMarginCompiler.lean` — the generic compiler and its split form.
- `LiteralWeilWindowSchurResidualBudget.lean` — `WindowSchurResidualBudgetData`, which
  converts the already-proved `|detRest| ≤ detRestBound` plus a budget into the old
  `hdom` hypothesis of the Schur gate.
- `LiteralWeilDetRestBoundMonotonicity.lean` — the endpoint copy of the quartic
  remainder polynomial and `detRestBound_mono`, the transport of ten nonnegative
  response bounds through the literal remainder.
- `NormalizedNarrowWindowCertificate.lean`, `WindowSchurSharedCertificate.lean` — the
  "construct the shared evidence once, keep downstream consumers thin" pattern:
  a single `NormalizedNarrowWindowTriple` carries the geometry, a single
  `ResponseEnvelope` carries the ten endpoint bounds, and one strict comparison
  generates `hdom`.
- `CertifiedFiniteCarrierReindex.lean` — the exact, presentation-only reindex boundary
  intended for the two-zero / three-taper stage (`∑ i, f i = ∑ j, f (e.symm j)`).
- `OddTaylorDeterminant.lean` — the exact odd cubic determinant algebra, including the
  double-radius coefficient `-36 r⁴`.

## What the Lean side then added on its own

The returned architecture was *empty* until a budget object was actually constructed.
It is now inhabited:

- `LiteralWeilNormalizedWindowBounds.lean` — unit-mass normalization and the geometric
  endpoint cap/floor for window responses;
- `LiteralWeilNarrowWindowDomination.lean` — the domination theorem: for all
  sufficiently narrow symmetric windows the endpoint budget is strictly below the cross
  margin, so `hdom` is discharged rather than assumed;
- `LiteralWeilWindowSchurInstance.lean` — an actual `NormalizedNarrowWindowTriple`, an
  actual `ResponseEnvelope`, an actual `WindowSchurResidualBudgetData`, and the first
  inhabited literal one-zero Schur admission.

